hi  welcome to another episode of cold  fusion where i cover anything in science  technology business or history  ai is encroaching on many fields that  are traditionally run by humans  so far it's been mostly technical tasks  recognizing ct scans for doctors  predicting oil deposits for engineers  image recognition tasks and things of  that sort but art is something different  and it's one of the last fields to be  encroached upon  art takes a unique combination of skill  creativity and the very human element of  aesthetic taste  but what if i told you that for the  first time it looks like the visual art  may have also fallen to ai  you all may have heard of the artificial  intelligence research group openai  in april of 2022 they released something  special it's a very powerful  text-to-image generator that can draw  virtually anything you want  this has been done before but the real  difference here is that the results will  almost always be artistically pleasing  by design with correct colors and  features that look like the creative  judgments of a real artist  in this episode we'll see exactly what  it can do how it works and what it means  for the world this is going to be an  interesting one let's get into it  [Music]  [Music]  so what is it  the new ai system is called dali 2 and  is capable of turning any text  description into a unique image or work  of art that has never been seen before  the technology is based on the gpt-3  text generation system which we've  talked about before  dali 2 is an updated version of dali  which we covered last year  the original daley was limited it could  only render images from text prompts in  a cartoonish manner  in contrast the new dali 2 system  generates high quality high resolution  images complete with complex backgrounds  depth of field effects realistic shadows  shading and reflections  the resolution is also higher  it also does its job much quicker only  taking about 10 seconds to generate  images it also includes new capabilities  like editing an existing image  so enough chatting let's see what it can  do  here we have the instagram artist karen  chang putting it through its  walking paces a staircase 10 seconds and  then it'll generate an image that's  brand new  [Music]  okay okay okay  that's pretty good um let me try  a girl walking up an infinity staircase  whoa  oh my god  these are beautiful  uh what kind of finished staircase made  of cookies in the clouds  [Music]  oh my god  [Applause]  look at this cookie staircase  yo this is like  the stuff of dreams yo  [Music]  so far some of you might be thinking so  what it's just smashing random images  together but as we'll see it's nothing  like that when you think about what's  going on here in a fundamental level  it's simply astounding  let's study a few examples to see what i  mean  look at this example of a dolphin in a  spacesuit  how did dali 2 know that that's how a  spacesuit on a dolphin would look like  why did it choose that pose  why is the lighting coming from that  particular direction why does the planet  have those particular colors  if you really think about it these are  generally the kind of decisions that an  artist would make  in this next example the prompt was to  make a napoleon cat holding cheese  again why did the system choose this  particular pose  how did it know that these colours would  work well in the scene look closely at  the details in the crease of the suit  and pants it all looks like it was  thoughtfully composed  in fact if you were the manager at a  cheese company you would pay an artist a  good amount of money to come up with a  concept like this for a poster in the  future this could be no more a user  could just type in a sentence and there  you go an instantly engaging  advertisement  the open ai team wrote about this  concept and called it filling in the  blanks  quote unlike a 3d rendering engine whose  inputs must be specified unambiguously  and in complete detail dali is often  able to fill in the blanks when the  caption implies that the image must  contain a certain detail that is not  explicitly stated  end quote this is significant because  uncertainty isn't something that  computers are traditionally good at  take a look at the rendering of this  front garden it looks almost as good as  a photo  let's take a look at some more examples  [Music]  [Music]  oh  is  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  uh  [Music]  she  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  of course serious artists may find this  lacking but for prototyping and concept  art this kind of power is immense  and just to see how far this has come  here is dali too compared with the  state-of-the-art ai just a year ago  sometimes the other ais can do it well  but a lot of the time the images seem  kind of muddled dali 2 in comparison  just seems more well thought out  i think you're probably starting to see  how big this is now  a lot of artists on twitter are starting  to show some concern  [Music]  so how does this work it just has to be  pulling from a data set somewhere and  just smashing objects together right  well no  as you saw you can type anything and it  will create it there are no specific  data sets of raccoons in space or  astronauts riding horses  there's no way that it's pulling from  data that's predefined in order to  generate these images the ai has to  mimic something akin to creativity and  come up with something original so how  does it do this  well it uses two main technologies built  by open ai the first is clip a computer  vision system and the second is gpt3 the  language model that can understand and  respond to human text as we've covered  in other episodes it can have  conversations understand context and  compose long passages of original text  it can even read and summarize  information for you  since we've already covered gpt3 at  length let's take a look at the first  component  clip stands for contrastive language  image pre-training the main idea behind  clip is to pre-train a natural language  model and an image classification model  simultaneously  it does this by using a lot of labelled  images that were extracted from the  internet  the clip system was able to look at a  given image and describe its contents in  a way that humans can understand  the open ai team essentially reversed  that process  instead of looking at an image and  describing its contents a user would  describe the contents and the ai would  build it  dali 2 actually generates the image  using a process called diffusion which  has been described by the company as  starting with a bag of dots and then  filling in a pattern with greater and  greater detail  diffusion is the hottest new method of  ai generation and can be a whole nother  topic for another day  but in order to save time let's move on  to something that i find a lot more  interesting the mimicking of human  preference  in order for the image outputs to be  pleasing to humans the open ai team  simply just modeled what humans would  find pleasing and integrated it into the  training process  they call this automated aesthetic  quality evaluations  to do this they generated 512 artistic  captions using gpt-3 next they trained  the system to predict human aesthetic  judgment  to do this they used the ava dataset  which is a large body of hand-labeled  video data  once trained the ai will output  resulting images that should always look  aesthetically pleasing to humans  to me it seems like this extra layer of  mimicking what humans would find  pleasing is making all the difference  so why stop there on this channel we've  already seen ai induce motion in still  images before so  why not add another layer that turns  these images into short video animations  in fact when you think about it the  possibilities with a system like this  are almost endless  so when saying all of this dali 2 is far  from perfect though sometimes it just  gets things plain wrong  give it the instruction a red cube on  top of a blue cube and will sometimes  mix up the order  okay so the comment section must be  exploding right now a lot of people  would have this next question on their  mind what about nefarious users couldn't  someone use this system to make a fake  photo of a famous person doing a  criminal act or produce generally  unsavory material  while open ai has thought of this and  has implemented some built-in safeguards  the model was trained on data that had  objectional material removed so there's  a good chance that the system would  struggle to produce anything  objectionable anyway  users are banned from uploading or  generating images that are quote not  g-rated and quote could cause harm  nudity obscene gestures or quote events  related to major ongoing political  events are all banned  additionally the system actively  prevents users from creating pictures  based on specific names  so images of celebrities public figures  and political leaders should be  impossible  open ai research scientist prafulla  dariwal states quote our hope is to keep  doing a staged process here so we can  keep evaluating from the feedback we get  on how to release this technology safely  understandably dali 2 is an extremely  powerful technology  so open ai is only sharing the software  with what it describes as a select  screened group of beta testers  even though open ai isn't releasing the  model to the public developers can look  at their technical findings in their  published papers and update their own  work  in a technical sense this makes sense  the ai research community is broadly  still trying to figure out what works  best  openai is basically telling the research  community hey we found that this method  seems to work extremely well this might  be the direction that ai research should  go in  researchers can sign up online and  preview the system  openai hopes to later make the system  available for third party apps  looking at their longer term goals alex  nicole one of the open ai researchers  who worked on the project says quote we  hope tools like this democratize the  ability for people to create whatever  they want he says that the tool could be  a useful product for designers magazine  cover designers and artists either for  use in inspiration and brainstorming or  to actually create finnish works  illya sutscova open ai's co-founder and  chief scientist said that dali 2 was an  important step towards open ai's goal of  creating artificial general intelligence  or agi  agi is a piece of software that can  achieve or exceed human performance in a  wide range of tasks  agi would need to process multi-modal  conceptual understanding being able to  associate a word with an image or a set  of images and vice versa  he states that dali 2 is an attempt to  create an ai with this sort of  understanding  for those of you who are interested and  want to tinker with dali too i'll leave  a link to the wait list in the  description below and to be clear this  episode was not sponsored  [Music]  so in conclusion only about five years  ago the idea that an ai could create art  from scratch was radical at that time  when we looked at it on this channel  most of you and me were both very  impressed but since then the technology  has absolutely skyrocketed it's no  longer a cute novelty it's grown up into  something that's starting to catch  people off guard in the following year  it will be twice as good and then the  year after four times as good soon when  we see custom animations generated only  by a text prompt people's minds will be  blown once again  right now and in the future we'll be  asking ourselves will this technology  make artists more powerful or will it  slowly chip away at their domain in fact  what is art is this still art if all the  human involvement during the creative  process is no more if the human creative  process is gone and a machine can mimic  it just as well what is true creativity  anyway  it's enough to make your head spin but  i'm gonna pass it off to you for now how  does this make you feel do you think  that this development is something  really interesting do you see spots of  opportunity or are you completely  terrified by this feel free to leave a  comment below i'd be interested to see  how your opinions range  so thanks for watching my name is dagogo  and you've been watching cold fusion if  you want to see anything else on science  technology business or history feel free  to look around on the channel i'll also  leave my ai playlist to learn more about  the insane progress being made behind  the scenes  also thanks to all of you who checked  out my new album on spotify when i  mentioned it in the end of the last  episode it means a lot anyway i'll catch  you again soon for the next episode  cheers guys  have a good one  uh  [Laughter]  [Music]  cold fusion  it's new thinking

